1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,973
Hi, we’re Sumit, Priyamvadha and Amiya.

2
00:00:02,973 --> 00:00:12,553
Our question: can the answers adults give on a health survey pick out people with diabetes or prediabetes, while missing as few of them as possible?

3
00:00:12,553 --> 00:00:22,133
We used the CDC’s 2015 BRFSS survey, about a quarter of a million adults, and followed an eight-stage roadmap. Each slide is labelled with its stage.

4
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:31,713
A questionnaire costs almost nothing, so if it can tell us who should get a blood test, screening reaches people who would otherwise never be tested.

5
00:00:31,713 --> 00:00:36,007
Just under 16% of these adults report diabetes or prediabetes.

6
00:00:36,007 --> 00:00:45,917
A missed case is the expensive mistake, so we fixed the target at catching 80% of cases and compare approaches by how many people they must flag,

7
00:00:45,917 --> 00:00:49,881
against the paper risk test clinics already hand out.

8
00:00:49,881 --> 00:00:59,130
Here is the roadmap we followed. Stage one checks the data and stage two cleans it. Stages three and four explore it and test associations.

9
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:04,746
Stage five sets up an honest evaluation, stage six trains and tunes the models,

10
00:01:04,746 --> 00:01:13,995
and stage seven chooses the screening cutoff and opens the test set once. Stage eight is this handoff. Every following slide carries its stage number.

11
00:01:13,995 --> 00:01:26,548
Stage 1 of 8: data ingestion and quality control.

12
00:01:26,548 --> 00:01:28,860
Stage one, data ingestion.

13
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:37,779
We loaded Kaggle’s version of the 2015 BRFSS and checked its shape, 253,680 rows by 22 columns, its column types, value ranges and labels,

14
00:01:37,779 --> 00:01:42,073
and recorded hashes so anyone can confirm the exact file.

15
00:01:42,073 --> 00:01:50,992
We used the three-class file because it separates prediabetes from diabetes, and we count both as positive: 15.8%. The roadmap’s 14% is diabetes alone.

16
00:01:50,992 --> 00:01:54,626
A sensitivity analysis looks at prediabetes by itself.

17
00:01:54,626 --> 00:02:06,518
Stage 2 of 8: preprocessing and cleaning.

18
00:02:06,518 --> 00:02:16,098
Stage two, cleaning. There were no missing or invalid codes, and the pipeline is built to stop rather than guess, so nothing was imputed or dropped.

19
00:02:16,098 --> 00:02:22,705
About 24,000 rows repeat someone else’s answers, but with 21 coarse answers different people often answer identically,

20
00:02:22,705 --> 00:02:26,669
so we kept them and tested their effect later.

21
00:02:26,669 --> 00:02:36,579
We sorted the answers into 14 yes/no, four ordered bands and three counts, and flagged unusual but valid answers, like a BMI over 60, without changing them.

22
00:02:36,579 --> 00:02:48,471
Stage 3 of 8: exploratory data analysis.

23
00:02:48,471 --> 00:02:56,399
Stage three, exploration. About one adult in six reports diabetes or prediabetes, so accuracy would mislead: always answering no scores 84%.

24
00:02:56,399 --> 00:03:02,345
People with the condition have a median BMI of 31 against 27 and are older.

25
00:03:02,345 --> 00:03:08,952
On the correlation heatmap, general health, high blood pressure, BMI, difficulty walking and high cholesterol stand out,

26
00:03:08,952 --> 00:03:14,568
and several of them move together, which is why we later adjust for them.

27
00:03:14,568 --> 00:03:26,460
Stage 4 of 8: hypotheses and statistics.

28
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:28,772
Stage four, hypothesis tests.

29
00:03:28,772 --> 00:03:36,700
We planned 25 tests: chi-square for the yes/no and ordered answers, and Mann–Whitney U for BMI, health days and the bands,

30
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,682
with Benjamini–Hochberg correction.

31
00:03:38,682 --> 00:03:46,940
All 25 were significant, which with a quarter of a million people says little, so we report effect sizes with confidence intervals.

32
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:53,547
High blood pressure goes with almost five times the odds; general health has the largest rank effect.

33
00:03:53,547 --> 00:03:58,832
The biggest odds ratio, for a cholesterol check, mostly measures contact with doctors.

34
00:03:58,832 --> 00:04:10,724
Stage 5 of 8: splitting and imbalance.

35
00:04:10,724 --> 00:04:13,367
Stage five, splitting and imbalance.

36
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:21,295
We fixed one stratified split for the whole team: 80% for training, divided into five fixed folds, and 20% locked away;

37
00:04:21,295 --> 00:04:30,875
the code refuses to release the test rows until a cutoff is saved. Scaling happens inside the model pipelines, so it only learns from training folds.

38
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:34,178
Repeated answers did not inflate the scores.

39
00:04:34,178 --> 00:04:42,767
And class weighting, compared with no weighting, didn’t rank people any better; it only inflated risks to 39%, so we kept honest probabilities.

40
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:54,659
Stage 6 of 8: training and tuning.

41
00:04:54,659 --> 00:04:57,302
Stage six, training and tuning.

42
00:04:57,302 --> 00:05:05,560
We tuned logistic regression as the interpretable baseline, a random forest and XGBoost, all on the same stratified folds, scored by PR-AUC.

43
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:15,470
The big jump is from the paper checklist to any model: to catch 80% of cases the checklist flags half of everyone, the models 38 to 39%.

44
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:21,086
Under the rule we wrote down first, XGBoost was chosen, winning all five folds.

45
00:05:21,086 --> 00:05:27,032
Logistic regression is close, if you need a model you can read line by line.

46
00:05:27,032 --> 00:05:30,666
Still stage six: what do the models use?

47
00:05:30,666 --> 00:05:38,264
Shuffling one answer at a time, all three agree on BMI, general health, age, high blood pressure and high cholesterol.

48
00:05:38,264 --> 00:05:45,862
Separately, a statsmodels regression with all 21 answers gives adjusted odds ratios with intervals, and it warns against one-at-a-time numbers:

49
00:05:45,862 --> 00:05:53,460
bad physical-health days reverse once general health is included, smoking’s association disappears and exercise mostly works through BMI and health.

50
00:05:53,460 --> 00:06:05,352
Stage 7 of 8: evaluation and thresholds.

51
00:06:05,352 --> 00:06:07,334
Stage seven, evaluation.

52
00:06:07,334 --> 00:06:14,601
We chose the cutoff on training data only: the saved model was refitted on four folds at a time,

53
00:06:14,601 --> 00:06:20,217
so every training person got a risk from a model that never saw them.

54
00:06:20,217 --> 00:06:28,475
The rule, written before the run: the highest cutoff that still catches 80% of cases. That is a predicted risk of 0.153.

55
00:06:28,475 --> 00:06:37,394
Calibration was already excellent, so no adjustment. Each extra ten points of sensitivity would cost roughly 90 to 130 more people flagged per thousand.

56
00:06:37,394 --> 00:06:45,983
Demo, step one: we try to score the test set before any cutoff exists. The command stops with an error and writes nothing.

57
00:06:45,983 --> 00:06:49,947
That lock is what keeps the final numbers honest.

58
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:56,884
Step two: the threshold command reads only the 202,944 training rows, refits the saved model fold by fold,

59
00:06:56,884 --> 00:07:00,518
and locks the cutoff at 0.1530 in threshold.json.

60
00:07:00,518 --> 00:07:07,455
Step three: with the cutoff saved, the test set is scored once, with 1,000 bootstrap resamples per model.

61
00:07:07,455 --> 00:07:14,392
XGBoost catches 79.4% of cases and flags 37.6% of people; the checklist flags 47.3% for the same cases.

62
00:07:14,392 --> 00:07:21,329
Finally, the committed model and cutoff score two made-up people: a healthy 35-year-old at 1% risk, not flagged,

63
00:07:21,329 --> 00:07:28,596
and a 65-year-old with high blood pressure at 60%, flagged. The full test suite passes: 63 tests, 98% coverage.

64
00:07:28,596 --> 00:07:32,560
The result on the 50,736 people we held back.

65
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:40,818
At the locked cutoff the model found 79.4% of people with diabetes or prediabetes, right on target, and flagged 37.6% of people;

66
00:07:40,818 --> 00:07:50,398
one in three of those flagged has the condition. Against the paper checklist, which finds the same share, that’s 96 fewer false alarms per thousand people.

67
00:07:50,398 --> 00:07:55,353
Precision, specificity, F2 and both AUCs match validation, so nothing was overfitted.

68
00:07:55,353 --> 00:07:58,987
These are the curves the roadmap asks for.

69
00:07:58,987 --> 00:08:06,254
On both the precision-recall and the ROC curve, the two models sit above the paper checklist at every cutoff,

70
00:08:06,254 --> 00:08:09,888
and the dots mark each locked operating point.

71
00:08:09,888 --> 00:08:17,486
On the right is calibration: predicted risks on the test set match the observed rates, with a slope of 0.99,

72
00:08:17,486 --> 00:08:21,780
so the model’s probabilities can be read as real risks.

73
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:27,065
In clinic terms: of every thousand people screened, about 158 have the condition.

74
00:08:27,065 --> 00:08:34,663
The model sends 376 for a blood test, finds 125 and misses 33; that’s the confusion matrix on the left.

75
00:08:34,663 --> 00:08:41,930
The paper checklist finds the same number but sends almost 100 more people, so its specificity is much lower.

76
00:08:41,930 --> 00:08:45,564
Logistic regression captures about 90% of the gain.

77
00:08:45,564 --> 00:08:47,876
Now the false negatives.

78
00:08:47,876 --> 00:08:57,125
The model leans on BMI, general health, age, blood pressure and cholesterol, so a case who looks ordinary on all five is invisible to it;

79
00:08:57,125 --> 00:09:02,410
the missed cases have the same average BMI as people without the condition.

80
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:08,356
Sensitivity is 44% for under-45s and 68% at incomes of 50 thousand dollars and up.

81
00:09:08,356 --> 00:09:15,293
And it finds 81% of diagnosed diabetes but only 65% of prediabetes, the group screening is really for.

82
00:09:15,293 --> 00:09:24,212
We also asked the harder question: leave out everyone who already has diabetes and look for prediabetes alone, with the same pipeline and rules.

83
00:09:24,212 --> 00:09:33,792
The model still beats the checklist, finding 81% of cases against 70%, but it flags 44% of everyone and only 4% of those flagged report prediabetes.

84
00:09:33,792 --> 00:09:38,747
Many of them may be undiagnosed cases, which this data can’t show.

85
00:09:38,747 --> 00:09:50,639
Stage 8 of 8: reporting and handoff.

86
00:09:50,639 --> 00:09:53,942
Stage eight includes being clear about limits.

87
00:09:53,942 --> 00:10:02,861
The labels are self-reported at one moment in 2015, so this finds people who already know their status; it predicts nothing about the future.

88
00:10:02,861 --> 00:10:11,450
88% of positives already have a diagnosis. Some strong answers partly measure seeing a doctor. One cutoff gives different groups very different trade-offs.

89
00:10:11,450 --> 00:10:17,396
The survey is unweighted and covers one year. And everything here is association, not cause.

90
00:10:17,396 --> 00:10:24,003
Our sources: the Kaggle and UCI versions of the data, the CDC’s MMWR article and NHANES portal,

91
00:10:24,003 --> 00:10:32,922
a historical NCHS report whose date should be checked before citing it as current, and the NIDDK risk test we used as the baseline.

92
00:10:32,922 --> 00:10:36,556
To sum up: with nothing but survey answers,

93
00:10:36,556 --> 00:10:45,805
a tuned model finds about eight in ten people with diabetes or prediabetes while sending a fifth fewer people for testing than the paper checklist.

94
00:10:45,805 --> 00:10:51,421
It is weaker for prediabetes and younger people, and it is a research prototype.

95
00:10:51,421 --> 00:11:00,000
Every stage is reproducible, backed by 63 automated tests, and the code, notebooks, reports and technical PDF are in our repository. Thank you.
